    {
      "id": "lesson_aotfw_008",
      "type": "lesson",
      "title": "Memory, Identity and Ono",
      "slug": "memory_identity_and_ono",
      "unit_id": "unit_aotfw",
      "sequence": 8,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "block_l008_001",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "heading",
          "variant": "page",
          "visibility": "student_teacher",
          "content": { "text": "Memory, Identity and Ono" },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_002",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "rich_text",
          "variant": "medium",
          "visibility": "student_teacher",
          "content": {
            "html": "<p>In this lesson we consolidate our reading of <em>An Artist of the Floating World</em> by examining how Masuji Ono's memories of the floating world, his wartime propaganda, and his role as a father combine to form an identity that is continually rewritten. Ishiguro does not present memory as a neutral archive; instead, recollection becomes a form of self-defence.</p><p>As you read, note moments where Ono contradicts his earlier account of the same event. These revisions are not errors—they are the novel's central evidence that identity is constructed through narrative.</p>"
          },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_003",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "callout",
          "variant": "medium",
          "visibility": "student_teacher",
          "content": {
            "style": "information",
            "title": "Focus question",
            "body": "How does Ishiguro use Ono's selective memory to explore the relationship between personal identity and national history?"
          },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_004",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "heading",
          "variant": "section",
          "visibility": "student_teacher",
          "content": { "text": "Close reading: the garden scene" },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_005",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "rich_text",
          "variant": "medium",
          "visibility": "teacher_only",
          "content": {
            "html": "<p>Teacher note: The garden conversation with Setsuko is the pivot for this lesson. Ono's insistence that his influence was 'small' directly contradicts evidence elsewhere in the text. Guide students to treat his tone—dignified, measured, slightly wounded—as part of the rhetorical strategy of minimisation.</p>"
          },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_006",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "callout",
          "variant": "medium",
          "visibility": "teacher_only",
          "content": {
            "style": "teacher",
            "title": "Facilitation notes",
            "body": "If students defend Ono as 'simply old and confused', push back with textual evidence of strategic omission. Ask: who benefits when a narrator controls the timeline? Connect to Module A: Textual Conversations—how does Ishiguro converse with histories Japan preferred to forget?"
          },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "block_l008_007",
          "type": "block",
          "block_type": "callout",
          "variant": "medium",
          "visibility": "student_teacher",
          "content": {
            "style": "extension",
            "title": "Extension",
            "body": "Compare Ono's self-narration with Stevens in Ishiguro's The Remains of the Day. Both narrators serve power while claiming personal dignity—what does this pairing suggest about Ishiguro's ongoing interest in memory and complicity?"
          },
          "layout": {},
          "print": {},
          "settings": {},
          "created_at": "2026-01-20T09:00:00.000Z",
          "updated_at": "2026-02-01T11:30:00.000Z",
          "schema_version": 1
        }
